  "reasoning_log_text": "The study primarily uses an auditory paradigm to distinguish conscious vs. nonconscious processing via local (within-trial) and global (across-trials) regularity violations. The 'local effect' is strongly associated with the MMN, suggesting automatic/nonconscious processing (H1). The 'global effect' is explicitly linked to conscious awareness of global regularity violations, which requires active counting or attention (H2/H3). Local effects were found in 100% of subjects, including VS patients (supporting H3/H2 aspects). The global effect was only present when subjects were conscious of the global structure (supporting H3/H2). The local effect's origin was localized to auditory cortices, while the global effect involved a 'brain-scale distributed network' (Global Workspace Network), supporting H3 ubiquity concepts. Specific quantitative findings on feedforward propagation, inhibition types (SST/PV), or specific frequency band activity were not detailed in the provided text."
